#!/bin/sh
# Refuse a history rewrite unless someone has deliberately taken the lock.
#
# Why this exists
# ---------------
# main was force-pushed twice in one session, and a background agent amended a
# commit that had already been pushed. The amend produced a new object, the
# branch moved, and every other session's base changed underneath it. Another
# agent ran `git reset` in the shared checkout, which orphaned a commit and
# discarded a second agent's uncommitted work mid-edit.
#
# None of that is a git bug. It is the predictable result of several sessions
# sharing one index and one working tree, which is why each session should have
# its own worktree. This hook is the backstop for whoever is still in the shared
# checkout.
#
# How it works
# ------------
# git feeds pre-push four fields per ref on stdin:
#
#   <local ref> <local sha> <remote ref> <remote sha>
#
# A ref is a history rewrite when the remote sha is non-zero and is NOT an
# ancestor of the local sha. Branch deletion (local sha zero) and a new branch
# (remote sha zero) are both fine and are excluded.
#
# How to override, when a rewrite is genuinely correct
# ---------------------------------------------------
#   VALENCE_ALLOW_FORCE_PUSH=1 git push --force-with-lease origin main
#
# Deliberately awkward. A rewrite should cost a deliberate act and leave a trace
# in the shell history, because the recovery cost lands on every other session
# and the benefit is almost always available through an ordinary commit.

zero=0000000000000000000000000000000000000000
rewrite=""

while read -r local_ref local_sha remote_ref remote_sha; do
    [ -z "$local_sha" ] && continue
    [ "$local_sha" = "$zero" ] && continue
    [ "$remote_sha" = "$zero" ] && continue

    if git merge-base --is-ancestor "$remote_sha" "$local_sha" 2>/dev/null; then
        continue
    fi

    rewrite="$rewrite  $local_ref
    local  $local_sha
    remote $remote_sha
"
done

if [ -z "$rewrite" ]; then
    exit 0
fi

if [ -n "$VALENCE_ALLOW_FORCE_PUSH" ]; then
    echo "VALENCE: history rewrite allowed by VALENCE_ALLOW_FORCE_PUSH." >&2
    echo "VALENCE: $rewrite" >&2
    echo "VALENCE: tell the other sessions the new base before you finish." >&2
    exit 0
fi

{
    echo "VALENCE: refused a non-fast-forward push."
    echo
    echo "$rewrite"
    cat <<'MSG'

This rewrites commits that are already on the remote, so every other session
working on this repository has to rebase onto the new tip. That has happened
twice already, and cost an orphaned commit plus discarded uncommitted work.

Normally the answer is an ordinary commit, which needs no override:

    git add -A && git commit -m "..." && git push

If the rewrite is genuinely correct, take the lock on purpose and say so out loud
so the other sessions can rebase:

    VALENCE_ALLOW_FORCE_PUSH=1 git push --force-with-lease origin main

MSG
} >&2

exit 1
